'en_content'=><<<'HTML'
<p>Pulses contain fibre, resistant starch and carbohydrates that are not completely digested in the small intestine. When they reach the colon, gut bacteria ferment them and produce gas. That means <strong>gas after chickpeas, beans or lentils does not automatically mean the food was badly cooked or is unhealthy</strong>.</p>
<p>Several practical measures can improve tolerance. Some change the preparation of the pulse itself; others change portion size and how often it is eaten.</p>
<h2>Why do pulses cause gas?</h2>
<p>Oligosaccharides, fibre and resistant starch are among the compounds involved. Human digestive enzymes cannot fully break all of them down before they reach the colon, where the microbiota uses them and gas is produced as a normal part of fermentation.</p>
<p>Sensitivity varies greatly between people and is also affected by how accustomed someone is to eating high-fibre foods.</p>
<h2>Soaking can help</h2>
<p>For chickpeas and many beans, soaking softens the seed, shortens cooking and allows some soluble compounds to move into the water. If digestive tolerance is the main goal, it usually makes sense to <strong>discard the soaking water, rinse the pulses and cook them in fresh water</strong>.</p>
<p>Small lentils often cook perfectly well without soaking, although a short soak may help some people.</p>
<h2>How long should they soak?</h2>
<p>There is no single universal time. Chickpeas and beans are commonly soaked for roughly 8 to 12 hours. In warm conditions, very long soaking at room temperature is not ideal; refrigeration is the safer option when the soak will be prolonged.</p>
<h2>Cook them until properly tender</h2>
<p>Undercooked pulses are not only unpleasantly firm but can also be harder to chew and tolerate. Chickpeas and beans should become tender through the centre. Cooking time depends on variety, age, water hardness and cooking method.</p>
<p>A pressure cooker can achieve a very soft texture efficiently and does not make pulses less digestible simply because it cooks them faster.</p>
<h2>Increase portions gradually</h2>
<p>One of the most useful strategies is to increase intake slowly. Someone who goes from little fibre to a very large bowl of beans is more likely to notice bloating. Smaller regular portions give the digestive system time to adapt.</p>
<h2>Does blending help?</h2>
<p>Hummus and purées change physical texture and can make eating easier, but <strong>blending does not remove fermentable carbohydrates</strong>. Some people tolerate the format better; others notice little difference.</p>
<h2>Do you need to remove the skins?</h2>
<p>The skins contain fibre. Removing them can make a smoother purée and may help certain individuals, but peeling every chickpea is not necessary for a healthy diet and also removes some fibre.</p>
<h2>What about cumin, fennel or bay?</h2>
<p>Traditional cuisines often use herbs and spices associated with digestion. They can improve flavour and some people find them helpful, but they should not be presented as a way to cancel all gas. Soaking when appropriate, thorough cooking and sensible portions matter more.</p>
<h2>Canned pulses</h2>
<p>Ready-cooked pulses are a convenient option. Draining and rinsing removes the packing liquid and can reduce sodium. If they are well tolerated, there is no requirement to cook pulses from dry every time.</p>
<h2>A practical strategy</h2>
<ol>
<li>Start with a moderate portion.</li>
<li>Soak chickpeas and beans.</li>
<li>Discard soaking water if digestive tolerance is the goal.</li>
<li>Rinse and cook in fresh water.</li>
<li>Cook until fully tender.</li>
<li>Eat smaller portions regularly rather than a huge serving occasionally.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>No method removes all gas because fermentation is partly a consequence of normal components of pulses. However, <strong>soaking, fresh cooking water, thorough cooking and gradual increases in intake</strong> are among the most useful ways to improve tolerance.</p>
